In the forward direction of continuity on maps, a relation of T(f) already holds for T(g i) at some index, when f is the pointwise supremum of a directed nonempty family g.

The proof is by induction on T. A constant keeps the relation and uses any index from nonemptiness. The variable is the given characterization of f. For a separated sum, the master clause uses any index, and a left or right tag uses the inductive hypothesis on that component. For a separated product, the two component relations give two indices, directedness supplies a common upper bound, and monotonicity of each component action transports the relations. For a coalesced sum, the master clause uses any index, and a proper left or right tag uses the corresponding inductive hypothesis, keeping the properness conditions. For a smash product, the master clause uses any index, and a proper rectangle uses both inductive hypotheses, a common upper bound, and monotonicity, keeping the properness conditions.
